ng  for additional data warehouse capacity  20th century fox uses bigquery to run  analytics to pull data from doubleclick  in youtube and uses tensorflow to do  audience analysis  on film scripts and track content  patterns that may  better predict what resonates with  audiences  hsbc needed to cost effectively upgrade  their aging territory environment  and across multiple regulatory  jurisdictions  by moving to bigquery they're able to  save millions over seven years  so how should you be thinking about your  data warehouse modernization strategy  this diagram shows a high level legacy  architecture from before the migration  it illustrates the catalog of available  data sources  legacy data pipelines legacy operational  pipelines and feedback loops  additionally legacy bi reports and the  dashboards that are  accessed by your end users in order to  migrate a legacy data warehouse  architecture to bigquery  we first need to go about decomposing  the migration such that we aren't left  with a  risky big bang cut over at google cloud  we generally think about data warehouse  migrations through the lens of the  following framework  first through cloud foundations where we  set the foundational cloud  infrastructure including network  i am and security then risk assessment  and mitigation where we evaluate  the operational risk of the migration  and then develop detailed mitigation  plans  third the migration where we actually  execute the migration  comprising of various steps then change  management and learning  setting up a center of excellence within  your organization such that  when the new environment is cut over  users are confident from day one  during the migration we generally  approach it from an iterative standpoint  first we'll assassin plan the migration  based on the source environment  and design the destination environment  then we'll work through an iterative  process  going workload by workload or subject  area by subject area  where we work on migrating those  workloads from the source system to the  destination system  this generally comprises of enterprise  setup and security mapping  migrating schema and data using bigquery  dts  translating queries using some of our  partner tools  migrating business apps migrating  upstream pipelines  verification and validation to ensure  that once the environment is cut over  that users are getting the same results  on top of the same data sets that they  had  when they had on their source system  performance tuning  and then of course report and scale  testing  now i'll turn it over to rob to take us  through how major league baseball  completed their migration journey  thank you ryan my name's rob goretsky  and i'm the vp of data engineering at  major league baseball  today i'm going to talk about major  league baseball's migration from  teradata to bigquery  including the motivations for migration  the actual migration process itself  and the capabilities we unlocked when we  completed this migration  so as a bit of a background i oversee  the fan data engineering and business  intelligence teams at major league  baseball  we work with fan transaction and  interaction data generated by internally  developed applications  including mlb.com and the 30 major  league baseball club websites  and our mlb and ballpark apps and third  party  sites such as ticketmaster tickets.com  stubhub and fanatics  my team doesn't work directly with  player stats or on field data such as  statcast  that's the domain of our baseball data  team at major league baseball  but we do work with the more traditional  user engagement data to ensure that we  understand our fans and can bring them  closer to the game of baseball  this includes personalizing and  targeting marketing and fan experiences  on our website  and on our apps and sharing our  centralized data with all 30 major  league baseball clubs  in our project known as wheelhouse  we have over 350 data pipelines that  have been developed  over the last 10 plus years most of our  data sets are not what i'd consider to  be really  big data for example a data set  containing every ticket sale  only contains about 80 million rows per  year for a couple hundred gigabytes of  data  that said we do have a few very large  data sets um  generated by user interactions on our  website and apps  these are highly trafficked and we  collect every user interaction and store  it in our data warehouse platform  in addition many of the medium-sized  data sets such as ticket sales  turn out to be pretty complicated in  terms of the modeling and validation  due to the various ways fans can  purchase exchange  resell transfer and ultimately share and  use these items  before our migration to bigquery we use  teradata as our data warehouse platform  we ran data pipelines using informatica  and airflow  mlb employees can access teradata  directly by connecting using sql and  using our bi platform looker  but we didn't have a means to open up  access directly to teradata for all 30  major league baseball clubs  without major performance and security  implications so we set up airflow  pipelines to essentially copy data  from teradata to s3 in 30 separate files  one per club  per data set to allow clubs to access  that data via s3 and athena  this setup presented several challenges  the major challenge was sharing data  with clubs  was that we were copying data so when  we're copying data you need to properly  identify which data has changed each day  if you want to do an incremental copy  and this was challenging in cases where  we had late arriving data  or corrections to make to the data that  happened often after the fact  which was especially common with our  ticketing data sets um  in addition if we wanted to evolve the  schema that we shared with clubs either  by adding fields or changing data types  that often would then require us to  recopy all historical data out to the  club which can be time consuming and  error-prone itself  in addition if we had a correction to  make we often would need to circulate  that on a change log so that clubs would  be aware that we've made a change to the  data  that they should refetch perhaps older  data files that we had published  um this process was also very much a  one-way street  clubs didn't have a way to share data  back with us in a way that we could  easily query  and that would automatically set up  schemas for us to work with  um as our data sources started to become  more and more real time  another issue with this setup was that  the system to share data was itself  batch based  so even if we were ingesting data in  real time to share the data back with  clubs involved making a copy  perhaps every hour or even more  frequently every five minutes  but we never really hit a real-time  cadence where clubs could see the data  as it happened  um other major challenges outside of the  data sharing  with working with teradata included the  operational support overhead  we maintained a full-time dba team who  helped us with the traditional dba tasks  such as  resolving performance issues monitoring  for database deadlocks and long-running  queries  resolving backup issues and we spent a  lot of time and effort trying to  continue to refine that part of the  system  when really that wasn't our core concern  our core concern was ultimately  providing  business data analytics to our  organization  with respect to performance we often  found that in spite of our best  efforts to implement proper workload  management and tuning  we often would have one bad apple  spoiling the bunch a single  well-intentioned user query  that ate up the entire spool space or  otherwise brought the system to its  knees  was fairly common and something that we  had to deal with all the time  um another issue as we started looking  at scaling our system on teradata was  that  the compute and storage were tightly  coupled this meant that if we wanted to  