wait for before replicating  data to bigquery but we consider this  critical for our success  because we wanted bigquery to be as  close as possible to being a perfect  match of what was in teradata  so for example if we get a daily load of  stubhub data  and that arrived into teradata at nine  o'clock am  we were hoping that we could have it in  bigquery by 901 am  so this is not a hot hot replication  it's not necessarily what you might  consider a real time replication  but it was basically close enough that  all of our analysts data scientists  report builders  bi team could immediately start using  bigquery very early on in the project  for all of their new work this ensured  basically that all the data was  essentially in bigquery as fresh as it  was in teradata  so as a result of this carefully timed  setup for replication  these data stakeholder teams were able  to start developing new queries and  processes on bigquery very early on  this was great because otherwise if  teams were continuing to build  new work on teradata during the seven  month migration  we might have another seven months worth  of migration to do afterwards and we  might never finish the project  so it was important for us that we could  sort of draw a line in the sand and say  to all of these stakeholder teams  only build new things on bigquery we're  going to replicate all the data you need  to work on your process so for example  for the data science team we're going to  make sure that all the ticket sales data  that you need  to do predictive analytics is there in  bigquery so it's going to look and feel  just like it does on teradata  um but be available to you at the same  time so that everything new you build  today you can start building on bigquery  in the back end we continued migrating  the etl jobs themselves  by simply having replication and unlock  the ability for most teams to think  about bigquery as a live production  system right away  as we converted etl jobs having  r