nces such as  function name changes or  small syntax changes could have been  resolved by a simple regex search and  replace  more complex cases involves having a  deeper understanding of the sql  structure and the data types that  underlie them  here's a really common case that we had  that used the teradata specific qualify  clause  the compilerworks tool was able to pull  the clause used within qualify  and rewrite the query as an inner query  doing this at hand would have been  error-prone at best  another major advantage we found when  using the compilerworks tool  is that it became aware of the actual  schema itself including the data types  so bigquery is pretty specific about  data types for example if a field  has a data type of integer it may not  allow you to directly insert a numeric  value without casting it to an integer  uh teradata was a bit more lacks about  this so this is the kind of thing that a  translation normally would trip up on  time and time again  the compiler works as tool being aware  of the data type of a column  when it parsed and read the sql it was  actually able to automatically apply the  proper cast or conversion statement to  it for us  so that the syntax would be valid and  would run immediately  when we were done converting an etl  pipeline we continue running the  original pipeline entire data  but we'd run the converted pipeline side  by side in a prod parallel environment  in this diagram here we see the airflow  dag diagram  of our original etl with teradata  operators embedded within  and on the right hand side we see the  exact same pipeline with bigquery  operators embedded within  and we set these up to run in two  separate environments but at generally  the same time of day with the same input  data  so that we could basically do a  side-by-side check that the output data  would match  to further assist us with this table  validation we looked in the marketplace  for tools that would essentially allow  us to run a diff between two tables  similar to how you run a diff when  you're using gif git to look at a  code review or pull request reviews we  couldn't find anything that fit our  needs exactly in terms of allowing us to  do  a table to table comparison um by  joining on the primary key  and then telling us if rows were missing  from one table or another  or if rows had fields that differed in  one table or another so we built our own  tool  our own tool essentially ran queries and  generated sql for us  on bigquery to take the output of the  replicated teradata etl  and compare that with the output of the  bigquery etl  this would quickly surface to us if  there were either differences  in the row count or in rows missing on  one side or the other  or if there were field differences at  this point this was one of the few  pieces of the project that really  required domain and analyst expertise  because if the diff tool showed  differences and it often would show some  differences  we need to make a business decision  about whether those differences in the  output were relevant for our use case  for example if there was a field that  simply had a created date or a time  stamp  we would expect those would be slightly  different between teradata and bigquery  simply because of the slightly different  runtime when those jobs completed  however if there was a field such as a  financial charge value  we would expect that generally those  should match up exactly  so if there was a difference in those  and it was significant we would need to  dig in further to see where in the  pipeline  something was either mistranslated or  something perhaps was uh thrown off for  some other reason  so having a tool like this allowed us to  have the data engineers spit out  conformed output that then we could pass  to analysts and end users  and more experienced users of the  business analytics side  to help us understand what the  differences were  so taking a look again at sort of where  we went we took a look before  at our data architecture before our  bigquery migration  including this middle layer where we had  um landing data  as a copy for club data access in our  wheelhouse project  after bigquery migration you see that  we've removed that middle layer  completely  consumers of data whether they are at  clubs  outside of our network or internal users  or our data science or other constituent  teams are all looking at the exact same  copy of the data there is not  a second copy made of any data this  means that if we load data in real time  it's available to those consumers in  real time  and it also means that we don't have to  deal with all the discrepancies that  arise by making copies of data  so we mentioned a few of the benefits  here and we're going to summarize them  here  with respect to democratization of data  i will do a live demo here in a few  moments  to show how we could use the bigquery  tools to very easily share data  and securely ensure that we only share a  subset of data with the proper  stakeholders  with respect to performance and scale we  saw that  some of our large queries had a 50  reduction in runtime without really  any additional work done on our end and  similarly we saw that certain data sets  such as our click stream data  which consist of over 300 terabytes a  year in uncompressed data  were able to be loaded into bigquery  whereas previously we couldn't even  consider doing something like that  so we instead needed to keep really  large data sets like those in separate  silos within a hadoop ecosystem to  process them  having them all in one place was one of  the single biggest user requests that we  had gotten  because users often want to join the  click stream data with other data sets  such as our ticketing or shop data  for security instead of having to have  separate user rights and roles and  credentials  all of this is centrally managed by our  infosec team by way of our integration  with octa and g suite  so users are set up with groups within  octa and those flow automatically into g  suite which then flows automatically  into bigquery roles and responsibilities  this means we don't have to have any  time spent on setting passwords worrying  about  who has compromised access who might  otherwise leave the organization  all of it is set up centrally in one  place  um developer happiness is really a key  one for me and my team  in that we just found that everybody who  worked with the system sort of  understood things right away with it and  generally had less gotchas than what  they did with teradata  um more specifically we found that one  of the biggest advantages is that we can  often  google if we're not sure about exactly  how to do something and find a helpful  stack overflow answer almost right away  this wasn't necessarily the case with  teradata uh which was generally the  domain of um  enterprise companies and larger  customers and really wasn't as widely  available  to the masses as something like bigquery  is where with bigquery there's a free  tier that you know any engineer can jump  in and try  things out with it it just means that  there's a wider mind share for it it  means that there's more documentation  and more community support for it  we find that helpful time and time again  uh with respect to features we're  finding that features are consistently  added that help us out  without breaking anything for us and  without requiring any downtime for  upgrades  um with teradata we were sort of pinned  to a specific version for several years  at a time  because an upgrade was a major process i  mean for us  with bigquery we just take a look at our  system one morning and we see release  notes and there's new features  and it's like opening a goodie bag you  immediately have some new toys to play  with every day  um so with respect to flexibility uh we  found that early on  um in our testing we didn't want to make  a big commitment about going with  big