ata  with the yankees in  assuming one doesn't already exist i can  go into bigquery's console  and simply set up a data set that we  will use for sharing with the yankees  and within that data set i'm going to  create a view and this is a standard sql  view  that's just going to say we're going to  create a view of next demo yankees  tickets as select star from  the parent data set where the  team name is the yankee so we're just  going to say where the team  name is equal to yankees so this is a  standard sql view  um and we'll see that it gets created  and we can check it out that the dbl  is available here for us and it's our  pretty traditional sql view  um where it just gives access to that  slice of the data  most data warehouse systems and database  systems do not allow you  to share access to the view without  sharing access to the underlying table  and this is where bigquery's authorized  view differs so  normally i couldn't share this view  without actually giving access to the  entire base table  which we wouldn't want to do because we  want to give the yankees only access to  their subset of the data  so all we have to do here to enable this  functionality is on  our parent data set set up the  authorized view to say that this new  view that we've created in the child  data set  is authorized to access the parent data  set so in this case here we're going to  say that the next demo yankees view is  authorized to access the next demo sales  the parent data set  with that in place all i now need to do  is figure out who i want to share the  data with  and this works very similarly to google  drive or  google docs sharing where you simply  provide a list of g suite email  addresses or g suite groups  for this demo i'm going to be using just  a single dummy  email address i set up but in production  we actually use  g suite groups to enable entire groups  of users at a time that are managed  locally by the clubs so all i have to do  to share data is give it