ng  for additional data warehouse capacity  20th century fox uses bigquery to run  analytics to pull data from doubleclick  in youtube and uses tensorflow to do  audience analysis  on film scripts and track content  patterns that may  better predict what resonates with  audiences  hsbc needed to cost effectively upgrade  their aging territory environment  and across multiple regulatory  jurisdictions  by moving to bigquery they're able to  save millions over seven years  so how should you be thinking about your  data warehouse modernization strategy  this diagram shows a high level legacy  architecture from before the migration  it illustrates the catalog of available  data sources  legacy data pipelines legacy operational  pipelines and feedback loops  additionally legacy bi reports and the  dashboards that are  accessed by your end users in order to  migrate a legacy data warehouse  architecture to bigquery  we first need to go about decomposing  the migration such that we aren't left  with a  risky big bang cut over at google cloud  we generally think about data warehouse  migrations through the lens of the  following framework  first through cloud foundations where we  set the foundational cloud  infrastructure including network  i am and security then risk assessment  and mitigation where we evaluate  the operational risk of the migration  and then develop detailed mitigation  plans  third the migration where we actually  execute the migration  comprising of various steps then change  management and learning  setting up a center of excellence within  your organization such that  when the new environment is cut over  users are confident from day one  during the migration we generally  approach it from an iterative standpoint  first we'll assassin plan the migration  based on the source environment  and design the destination environment  then we'll work through an iterative  process  going workload by workload or subject  area by subject area  where we work on migrating those  workloads from the source system to the  destination system  this generally comprises of enterprise  setup and security mapping  migrating schema and data using bigquery  dts  translating queries using some of our  partner tools  migrating business apps migrating  upstream pipelines  verification and validation to ensure  that once the environment is cut over  that users are getting the same results  on top of the same data sets that they  had  when they had on their source system  performance tuning  and then of course report and scale  testing  now i'll turn it over to rob to take us  through how major league baseball  completed their migration journey  thank you ryan my name's rob goretsky  and i'm the vp of data engineering at  major league baseball  today i'm going to talk about major  league baseball's migration from  teradata to bigquery  including the motivations for migration  the actual migration process itself  and the capabilities we unlocked when we  completed this migration  so as a bit of a background i oversee  the fan data engineering and business  intelligence teams at major league  baseball  we work with fan transaction and  interaction data generated by internally  developed applications  including mlb.com and the 30 major  league baseball club websites  and our mlb and ballpark apps and third  party  sites such as ticketmaster tickets.com  stubhub and fanatics  my team doesn't work directly with  player stats or on field data such as  statcast  that's the domain of our baseball data  team at major league baseball  but we do work with the more traditional  user engagement data to ensure that we  understand our fans and can bring them  closer to the game of baseball  this includes personalizing and  targeting marketing and fan experiences  on our website  and on our apps and sharing our  centralized data with all 30 major  league baseball clubs  in our project known as wheelhouse  we have over 350 data pipelines that  have been developed  over the last 10 plus years most of our  data sets are not what i'd consider to  be really  big data for example a data set  containing every ticket sale  only contains about 80 million rows per  year for a couple hundred gigabytes of  data  that said we do have a few very large  data sets um  generated by user interactions on our  website and apps  these are highly trafficked and we  collect every user interaction and store  it in our data warehouse platform  in addition many of the medium-sized  data sets such as ticket sales  turn out to be pretty complicated in  terms of the modeling and validation  due to the various ways fans can  purchase exchange  resell transfer and ultimately share and  use these items  before our migration to bigquery we use  teradata as our data warehouse platform  we ran data pipelines using informatica  and airflow  mlb employees can access teradata  directly by connecting using sql and  using our bi platform looker  but we didn't have a means to open up  access directly to teradata for all 30  major league baseball clubs  without major performance and security  implications so we set up airflow  pipelines to essentially copy data  from teradata to s3 in 30 separate files  one per club  per data set to allow clubs to access  that data via s3 and athena  this setup presented several challenges  the major challenge was sharing data  with clubs  was that we were copying data so when  we're copying data you need to properly  identify which data has changed each day  if you want to do an incremental copy  and this was challenging in cases where  we had late arriving data  or corrections to make to the data that  happened often after the fact  which was especially common with our  ticketing data sets um  in addition if we wanted to evolve the  schema that we shared with clubs either  by adding fields or changing data types  that often would then require us to  recopy all historical data out to the  club which can be time consuming and  error-prone itself  in addition if we had a correction to  make we often would need to circulate  that on a change log so that clubs would  be aware that we've made a change to the  data  that they should refetch perhaps older  data files that we had published  um this process was also very much a  one-way street  clubs didn't have a way to share data  back with us in a way that we could  easily query  and that would automatically set up  schemas for us to work with  um as our data sources started to become  more and more real time  another issue with this setup was that  the system to share data was itself  batch based  so even if we were ingesting data in  real time to share the data back with  clubs involved making a copy  perhaps every hour or even more  frequently every five minutes  but we never really hit a real-time  cadence where clubs could see the data  as it happened  um other major challenges outside of the  data sharing  with working with teradata included the  operational support overhead  we maintained a full-time dba team who  helped us with the traditional dba tasks  such as  resolving performance issues monitoring  for database deadlocks and long-running  queries  resolving backup issues and we spent a  lot of time and effort trying to  continue to refine that part of the  system  when really that wasn't our core concern  our core concern was ultimately  providing  business data analytics to our  organization  with respect to performance we often  found that in spite of our best  efforts to implement proper workload  management and tuning  we often would have one bad apple  spoiling the bunch a single  well-intentioned user query  that ate up the entire spool space or  otherwise brought the system to its  knees  was fairly common and something that we  had to deal with all the time  um another issue as we started looking  at scaling our system on teradata was  that  the compute and storage were tightly  coupled this meant that if we wanted to  consider bringing in some of the very  very large data sets such as our  clickstream  we couldn't do so without also linearly  increasing our compute  which came at much additional cost that  perhaps didn't make sense for us overall  um furthermore we found that integration  with third-party tools and apps was  limited  when we were looking to integrate with  new bi platforms or data science tools  or analytics tools  often their support for teradata was  mediocre at best  so we ran a project to fully migrate  terry data from bigquery  from may through november 2019 so we ran  this entire project in about seven  months time  at a high level the work involved  setting up data replications from  teradata to bigquery  and then iterating through the  conversion of etl jobs and reports  we needed to continue to support live  reporting and agreed  that a big bang cutover happening all at  once was not going to be feasible or  desirable for us  we instead wanted to take things in  chunks and ensure that along the way we  were validating that bigquery was  performing as expected  and that data was loading properly and  matching up with our expectations  so in order to determine the order in  which to attack  converting and migrating all of our etl  jobs  we built a dependency map that took a  look at which tables and etl jobs were  upstream and downstream from each other  jobs that were less entangled because  they were on the leaf nodes in this  diagram  had fewer downstream dependencies and  therefore could be migrated first  so we worked outside in if you will on  this diagram where we took those things  that had the fewest downstream  dependencies  um migrated them first and then  continued to work our way inwards  we set up hundreds of replication jobs  using apache airflow very early on in  our project  and throughout the project as needed as  new tables arose  one of the more time-consuming tasks  about this was determining exactly which  upstream process  we should wait for before replicating  data to bigquery but we consider this  critical for our success  because we wanted bigquery to be as  close as possible to being a perfect  match of what was in teradata  so for example if we get a daily load of  stubhub data  and that arrived into teradata at nine  o'clock am  we were hoping that we could have it in  bigquery by 901 am  so this is not a hot hot replication  it's not necessarily what you might  consider a real time replication  but it was basically close enough that  all of our analysts data scientists  report builders  bi team could immediately start using  bigquery very early on in the project  for all of their new work this ensured  basically that all the data was  essentially in bigquery as fresh as it  was in teradata  so as a result of this carefully timed  setup for replication  these data stakeholder teams were able  to start developing new queries and  processes on bigquery very early on  this was great because otherwise if  teams were continuing to build  new work on teradata during the seven  month migration  we might have another seven months worth  of migration to do afterwards and we  might never finish the project  so it was important for us that we could  sort of draw a line in the sand and say  to all of these stakeholder teams  only build new things on bigquery we're  going to replicate all the data you need  to work on your process so for example  for the data science team we're going to  make sure that all the ticket sales data  that you need  to do predictive analytics is there in  bigquery so it's going to look and feel  just like it does on teradata  um but be available to you at the same  time so that everything new you build  today you can start building on bigquery  in the back end we continued migrating  the etl jobs themselves  by simply having replication and unlock  the ability for most teams to think  about bigquery as a live production  system right away  as we converted etl jobs having  replication in place also allowed us to  ensure  that we could do table validation and  match results between the result of  output from teradata and bigquery  so in order to proceed with etl  conversion we focused on developing a  recipe that can be followed  by data engineers without them having  advanced knowledge  of any of the business or domain logic  that were contained within that pipeline  so we started off looking at steps that  can be automated  and we spend some time upfront  developing tooling to automate those  processes  for example we wrote shellers python  scripts that would simply pull pieces of  sql  out of parts of our code so that we  could quickly identify which pieces  needed to be translated  we also looked for cases where there  were teradata specific function calls or  teradata specific  commands being issued and we were able  to pull those out and kind of translate  those in mass  so this meant that for the first month  or so of our project we weren't  necessarily doing an end-to-end etl  conversion just yet  but we were kind of building out the  tools that would enable us to then move  very quickly through all of the others  so once we got through building out a  lot of the automation the remaining  steps was a majority of the project and  it was done by our data engineers  this involved places where either it was  too difficult to build automation for  something  or because we wanted to have validation  done by a human  who could look over something and ensure  that it passed proper sanity checks  um also working with informatica we  found that there was no easy way to  automate  you know mass changes so a lot of those  involved clicking through the interface  and just manually making changes in the  gui  so with respect to automated components  one tool that helped us immensely was a  sql transpiler tool  by a company called compilerworks this  tool accepts  teradata sql as input and then outputs  bigquery sql  so while minor sql differences such as  function name changes or  small syntax changes could have been  resolved by a simple regex search and  replace  more complex cases involves having a  deeper understanding of the sql  structure and the data types that  underlie them  here's a really common case that we had  that used the teradata specific qualify  clause  the compilerworks tool was able to pull  the clause used within qualify  and rewrite the query as an inner query  doing this at hand would have been  error-prone at best  another major advantage we found when  using the compilerworks tool  is that it became aware of the actual  schema itself including the data types  so bigquery is pretty specific about  data types for example if a field  has a data type of integer it may not  allow you to directly insert a numeric  value without casting it to an integer  uh teradata was a bit more lacks about  this so this is the kind of thing that a  translation normally would trip up on  time and time again  the compiler works as tool being aware  of the data type of a column  when it parsed and read the sql it was  actually able to automatically apply the  proper cast or conversion statement to  it for us  so that the syntax would be valid and  would run immediately  when we were done converting an etl  pipeline we continue running the  original pipeline entire data  but we'd run the converted pipeline side  by side in a prod parallel environment  in this diagram here we see the airflow  dag diagram  of our original etl with teradata  operators embedded within  and on the right hand side we see the  exact same pipeline with bigquery  operators embedded within  and we set these up to run in two  separate environments but at generally  the same time of day with the same input  data  so that we could basically do a  side-by-side check that the output data  would match  to further assist us with this table  validation we looked in the marketplace  for tools that would essentially allow  us to run a diff between two tables  similar to how you run a diff when  you're using gif git to look at a  code review or pull request reviews we  couldn't find anything that fit our  needs exactly in terms of allowing us to  do  a table to table comparison um by  joining on the primary key  and then telling us if rows were missing  from one table or another  or if rows had fields that differed in  one table or another so we built our own  tool  our own tool essentially ran queries and  generated sql for us  on bigquery to take the output of the  replicated teradata etl  and compare that with the output of the  bigquery etl  this would quickly surface to us if  there were either differences  in the row count or in rows missing on  one side or the other  or if there were field differences at  this point this was one of the few  pieces of the project that really  required domain and analyst expertise  because if the diff tool showed  differences and it often would show some  differences  we need to make a business decision  about whether those differences in the  output were relevant for our use case  for example if there was a field that  simply had a created date or a time  stamp  we would expect those would be slightly  different between teradata and bigquery  simply because of the slightly different  runtime when those jobs completed  however if there was a field such as a  financial charge value  we would expect that generally those  should match up exactly  so if there was a difference in those  and it was significant we would need to  dig in further to see where in the  pipeline  something was either mistranslated or  something perhaps was uh thrown off for  some other reason  so having a tool like this allowed us to  have the data engineers spit out  conformed output that then we could pass  to analysts and end users  and more experienced users of the  business analytics side  to help us understand what the  differences were  so taking a look again at sort of where  we went we took a look before  at our data architecture before our  bigquery migration  including this middle layer where we had  um landing data  as a copy for club data access in our  wheelhouse project  after bigquery migration you see that  we've removed that middle layer  completely  consumers of data whether they are at  clubs  outside of our network or internal users  or our data science or other constituent  teams are all looking at the exact same  copy of the data there is not  a second copy made of any data this  means that if we load data in real time  it's available to those consumers in  real time  and it also means that we don't have to  deal with all the discrepancies that  arise by making copies of data  so we mentioned a few of the benefits  here and we're going to summarize them  here  with respect to democratization of data  i will do a live demo here in a few  moments  to show how we could use the bigquery  tools to very easily share data  and securely ensure that we only share a  subset of data with the proper  stakeholders  with respect to performance and scale we  saw that  some of our large queries had a 50  reduction in runtime without really  any additional work done on our end and  similarly we saw that certain data sets  such as our click stream data  which consist of over 300 terabytes a  year in uncompressed data  were able to be loaded into bigquery  whereas previously we couldn't even  consider doing something like that  so we instead needed to keep really  large data sets like those in separate  silos within a hadoop ecosystem to  process them  having them all in one place was one of  the single biggest user requests that we  had gotten  because users often want to join the  click stream data with other data sets  such as our ticketing or shop data  for security instead of having to have  separate user rights and roles and  credentials  all of this is centrally managed by our  infosec team by way of our integration  with octa and g suite  so users are set up with groups within  octa and those flow automatically into g  suite which then flows automatically  into bigquery roles and responsibilities  this means we don't have to have any  time spent on setting passwords worrying  about  who has compromised access who might  otherwise leave the organization  all of it is set up centrally in one  place  um developer happiness is really a key  one for me and my team  in that we just found that everybody who  worked with the system sort of  understood things right away with it and  generally had less gotchas than what  they did with teradata  um more specifically we found that one  of the biggest advantages is that we can  often  google if we're not sure about exactly  how to do something and find a helpful  stack overflow answer almost right away  this wasn't necessarily the case with  teradata uh which was generally the  domain of um  enterprise companies and larger  customers and really wasn't as widely  available  to the masses as something like bigquery  is where with bigquery there's a free  tier that you know any engineer can jump  in and try  things out with it it just means that  there's a wider mind share for it it  means that there's more documentation  and more community support for it  we find that helpful time and time again  uh with respect to features we're  finding that features are consistently  added that help us out  without breaking anything for us and  without requiring any downtime for  upgrades  um with teradata we were sort of pinned  to a specific version for several years  at a time  because an upgrade was a major process i  mean for us  with bigquery we just take a look at our  system one morning and we see release  notes and there's new features  and it's like opening a goodie bag you  immediately have some new toys to play  with every day  um so with respect to flexibility uh we  found that early on  um in our testing we didn't want to make  a big commitment about going with  bigquery  but we love that we could use the  on-demand model to load some data even  some of our larger data sets  and try things out without having to  make any upfront commitment  um the operational overhead has been  significantly reduced  right now backups are automatic and one  of the things that our team really loves  is the ability to simply ask bigquery to  present a copy of a table anytime in the  last seven days  so if you're trying to validate a data  load issue that happened last night you  can simply ask bigquery to  present the copy of the table from  yesterday and you could take a look at  what changed  similarly if you've ever accidentally  dropped a production table and i know  none of us have  ever done that uh you could easily  undelete a table for up to three days  with a single sql command so there's no  need to run to the dba and ask for a  tape back up and you know have your  heart skip a beat as you think about the  implications of what's happened there  um bigquery kind of handles all of that  for us in a really easy  to use way um and finally with respect  to cost  again the on-demand pricing allowed us  to start things up  at a low run rate and then once we found  what we stabilized at we could basically  purchase reserve slots  and make sure that our cost was fixed  going forward that we wouldn't have  sudden spikes  so now i'm going to jump into a live  demo where i'm going to show how we  democratize data at mlb  and in this case i'm going to walk  through an example where we are going to  take a data set that contains data for  all 30 major league baseball clubs  and show how he would share it with just  one of those clubs uh while applying  security  to ensure that that club can only see  their own data i'm  going to jump in from the perspective of  a single club in this case the yankees  and show how that club is able to access  that data um  and even run queries with their own data  combining and joining with that data  to demonstrate how bigquery's data set  sharing and authorized views allowed us  to democratize data access at major  league baseball  i'm going to walk through a small  example here  i'm in the bigquery console logged in as  a major league baseball employee  so i have access to data across all 30  teams  for example here i've set up a small  dummy schema  of a tickets fact table that contains  one row per ticket sale transaction  as major league baseball we have access  to all 30 teams data  and one of our goals here is to ensure  that we share that data with each of the  30 clubs so that they can make their own  local business decisions  so the goal would be to share the giants  data with just the giants and the white  sox data with just the white sox  in our first iteration of wheelhouse  data sharing we accomplished this  by setting up 30 copies of the data each  day so we would run queries  to segment out the data and pull all of  the giant's data and put that in one  file  then we'd pull data for the white sox  and put that in another file this  involved making copies of the data which  incurs issues when perhaps you have data  that has been updated later on after the  file was produced perhaps  other data synchronization issues and  just other challenges in  making sure that you stay in sync with  what you've copied out so with bigquery  this gets a lot easier  so to demonstrate this first of all  logged in as major league baseball i can  do a demo query to show  that i can run something like a sum of  the quantity  um from this table across all 30 teams  so as major league baseball  i can see the sum of the quantity broken  down by the team name for example  um and we can see that i do have access  here as major league baseball to all 30  of the club's data  so now if i want to just share data with  the yankees for example we should be  giving them access to just the slice of  the data pertinent to them  the way we accomplish that is by  creating a data set that we share data  with the yankees in  assuming one doesn't already exist i can  go into bigquery's console  and simply set up a data set that we  will use for sharing with the yankees  and within that data set i'm going to  create a view and this is a standard sql  view  that's just going to say we're going to  create a view of next demo yankees  tickets as select star from  the parent data set where the  team name is the yankee so we're just  going to say where the team  name is equal to yankees so this is a  standard sql view  um and we'll see that it gets created  and we can check it out that the dbl  is available here for us and it's our  pretty traditional sql view  um where it just gives access to that  slice of the data  most data warehouse systems and database  systems do not allow you  to share access to the view without  sharing access to the underlying table  and this is where bigquery's authorized  view differs so  normally i couldn't share this view  without actually giving access to the  entire base table  which we wouldn't want to do because we  want to give the yankees only access to  their subset of the data  so all we have to do here to enable this  functionality is on  our parent data set set up the  authorized view to say that this new  view that we've created in the child  data set  is authorized to access the parent data  set so in this case here we're going to  say that the next demo yankees view is  authorized to access the next demo sales  the parent data set  with that in place all i now need to do  is figure out who i want to share the  data with  and this works very similarly to google  drive or  google docs sharing where you simply  provide a list of g suite email  addresses or g suite groups  for this demo i'm going to be using just  a single dummy  email address i set up but in production  we actually use  g suite groups to enable entire groups  of users at a time that are managed  locally by the clubs so all i have to do  to share data is give it